Deep neural network training for semantic segmentation requires considerable effort,
so many researchers opt for pre-training their model on a different task before
further refining it using a smaller annotated dataset. Top-notch approaches
traditionally rely on image classification or object detection for pre-training,
though these methods have drawbacks: they often fail to account for a scene's
spatial layout essential for segmentation or are hindered by sparse object labels.
Recent depth estimation techniques using monocular or stereo images have shown
promising results, so we query whether these methods can serve as effective
pre-training options. Our novel approach is unique in several respects: it's
the first to evaluate pre-training for semantic segmentation using monocular depth
estimation, and it's also the first to combine pre-training with joint training
on a small dataset. Our experimental findings indicate that monocular depth
estimation can surpass image classification in pre-training, and our proposed
approach surpasses both classification- and object-detection-based pre-training
methods while outperforming traditional techniques that train directly from
large semantic segmentation datasets. Notably, our approach is more efficient and
requires no augmented datasets. Our code is publicly available on GitHub. 
