Handwritten Text Recognition (HTR) with limited resources is a complex challenge, largely due to the scarcity of high-quality annotated data in under-resourced languages. To overcome this obstacle, we present a novel data generation approach, combing unconstrained writing inputs with predefined word meanings to facilitate one-shot learning. Focusing on Latin-based languages, we develop a system to create comprehensive gesture templates for words, leveraging a dictionary-based word segmentation method to ensure accuracy. To enhance the realism of generated scripts, we incorporate a sequence-to-sequence model trained on English handwriting transcriptions. Moreover, to boost the diversity of generated data, we implement a bi-directional auto-encoder model. To validate our method, we conduct experiments on datasets from three under-resourced languages, demonstrating that our approach yields one-shot performance equivalent to training with 1000-shot datasets using existing conventional techniques. Additionally, a user study confirms the produced data's readability and writing accuracy. To further empower the global community, we open-source our code and dataset generation tools. Further details are available on our project website. 

